      "writtenOn": "2023-09-13T17:04:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From the documentation:\n```\nThe C string is allocated in the C heap using malloc.\nIt is the caller\u0027s responsibility to arrange for it to be freed.\n```\nI don\u0027t see bridge::getSentences call free on text unless `gUnicode-\u003egetSentences()` takes ownership of the string (which would be a strange API decision IMO).\n\nI have the same concerns for all calls to C.CString in this file.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 54,
        "startChar": 31,
        "endLine": 54,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "0605b22a3741ceec121194cbeb735fd187e2c440",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2aed4909_f2512792",
        "filename": "tools/unicode_comparison/go/bridge.go",
        "patchSetId": 55
      },
      "lineNbr": 59,
      "author": {
        "id": 5395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-13T17:04:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not a huge fan of having a global block of memory in the C++ side (gSentences) and then having to be careful to copy the data out of it using reflection.\n\nAssuming we have a reasonable upperbound of points that getSentences can return, we can declare a large array (or slice)\n\n```\nvar sentenceData uint[10000]\n\nfunc GetSentences(text string) []uint {\n\tlength :\u003d C.getSentences(C.CString(text), unsafe.Pointer(sentenceData))\n\tvar result \u003d make([]uint, length)\n\tcopy(result, sentenceData[:length])\n\treturn result\n}\n```\n\nWould something like that work ok or do we *need* the dynamic allocation?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 59,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 59,
        "endChar": 8
      },
      "revId": "0605b22a3741ceec121194cbeb735fd187e2c440",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
